WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.995
Euclid's Elements, Book One, Proposition Thirty-Six.

2
00:00:03.995 --> 00:00:09.081
Parallelograms on equal bases and in the same parallels are equal in area.

3
00:00:09.081 --> 00:00:17.262
Let parallelograms A B C D and E F G H stand on equal bases B C and F G and lie between the same parallels A H and B G.

4
00:00:17.262 --> 00:00:21.730
We prove that parallelogram A B C D equals parallelogram E F G H.

5
00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:26.829
Construct segments B E and C H joining points B and C to points E and H.

6
00:00:26.829 --> 00:00:38.200
Since base B C equals base F G, and by Proposition Thirty-Four opposite side F G equals E H in parallelogram E F G H, Common Notion One gives segment B C equal to segment E H.

7
00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:47.132
Segments B C and E H are both equal and parallel, and segments E B and H C join their extremities in the same directions.

8
00:00:47.132 --> 00:00:55.714
By Proposition Thirty-Three, segments E B and H C are themselves equal and parallel, so figure E B C H is a parallelogram.

9
00:00:55.714 --> 00:01:06.196
By Proposition Thirty-Five, parallelogram E B C H equals parallelogram A B C D, since they share the same base B C between the parallels B C and A H.

10
00:01:06.196 --> 00:01:13.512
For the same reason, parallelogram E F G H equals the same parallelogram E B C H on the shared base E H.

11
00:01:13.512 --> 00:01:21.180
Therefore by Common Notion One, parallelogram A B C D equals parallelogram E F G H, as was required to prove.
